@platform=http://localhost:7060
@host=http://127.0.0.1:7071
@firstWorkerProxy=http://localhost:7081
@secondWorkerProxy=http://localhost:7082

# Multi-worker scenario: worker1 serves -> link worker2 later -> both serve on the SAME Host.
# In Visual Studio, select the AppHost profile `project-placeholder-manual-multi-worker` and press F5.
# Or run from the repository root:
# dotnet run --project tools\ComputeSeparation\AppHost\ComputeSeparation.AppHost.csproj --launch-profile project-placeholder-manual-multi-worker
#
# Stop any previous AppHost before starting: these manual profiles share fixed ports.
# Confirm Aspire shows worker-proxy / isolated-worker AND worker-proxy-2 / isolated-worker-2.
# Both worker processes start with Aspire; assignment and linking are manual.
# This file is self-contained: do not run the single-worker file first.
# /simulate routes belong to FakePlatform; it calls the real Host and WorkerProxy contracts.

# 1. Assign worker1
# Expect 201 (204 if repeated). Retry 503 WorkerNotReady after the worker connects.
# @name assignFirstWorker
POST {{platform}}/simulate/worker/assign?worker=first

### 2. Assign the placeholder Host to the app
# Expect 202 (409 if repeated). Specialization applies configuration without waiting for a worker link.
# @name assignHost
POST {{platform}}/simulate/host/assign

### 3. Link worker1
# Expect 201 (200 for a matching retry).
# @name linkFirstWorker
POST {{platform}}/simulate/worker/link?worker=first

### 4. Confirm worker1 serves BEFORE linking worker2
# Wait for 200 before continuing. Note X-Compute-Worker-Id in the response headers.
# @name invokeBeforeLaterLink
GET {{host}}/api/hello

### Optional: confirm capacity after worker1 is ready
# Expect { "createdTime": "<ISO8601 UTC timestamp>", "httpCapacity": 16, "workerCount": 1 }. Each ready HTTP worker contributes a fixed 16.
# Retry while capacity is 0; 204 means the Host has not published yet.
# Retries reuse createdTime; distinct snapshots may have equal or earlier timestamps, not strictly increasing times.
# FakePlatform ignores strictly older times with 200 and accepts ties in arrival order, not causal order.
# Clock rollback can leave a legitimate update ignored; timestamps do not guarantee ordering across restarts.
GET {{platform}}/simulate/host/state

### 5. Assign worker2 independently
# Expect 201 (204 if repeated). Retry 503 WorkerNotReady after the worker connects.
# 400 with worker-selection guidance: check that the multi-worker profile is running.
# The single-worker profile does not configure worker2; retrying alone cannot enable it.
# @name assignSecondWorker
POST {{platform}}/simulate/worker/assign?worker=second

### 6. Link worker2 to the already-running Host
# Expect 201. Do not restart the Host between steps 4 and 6.
# @name linkSecondWorker
POST {{platform}}/simulate/worker/link?worker=second

### Optional: confirm capacity after worker2 is ready
# Expect httpCapacity: 32 and workerCount: 2 once both workers finish loading. Record createdTime before the duplicate link.
GET {{platform}}/simulate/host/state

### 7. Repeat worker2's link to check idempotency
# Expect 200: same channel, no function reload on existing workers.
# @name repeatSecondLink
POST {{platform}}/simulate/worker/link?worker=second

### Optional: confirm the duplicate link does not change capacity
# Expect the same createdTime, httpCapacity: 32, and workerCount: 2; duplicate links do not create snapshots.
# Accepted snapshots are not periodically resent.
GET {{platform}}/simulate/host/state

### 8. Invoke after the later link
# Expect 200. Send steps 8 and 9 repeatedly after worker2 finishes loading.
# X-Compute-Worker-Id should show both identities; worker2's ID has the "-2" suffix.
# Selection is round-robin among ready workers, not targeted by the request.
# @name invokeAfterLaterLinkA
GET {{host}}/api/hello

### 9. Invoke again and compare worker identity
# Expect 200 and the other worker's X-Compute-Worker-Id when both are ready and no other traffic is running.
# @name invokeAfterLaterLinkB
GET {{host}}/api/hello

### Optional diagnostics: worker1 pod state
# Expect ReadyForRequest after assignment. Aspire provides separate logs for each proxy and worker.
GET {{firstWorkerProxy}}/admin/worker/state

### Optional diagnostics: worker2 pod state
GET {{secondWorkerProxy}}/admin/worker/state
